From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: a missing vendor image loads the device tree's crust

The release runs the vendor ARISC firmware by default (decision of
22.10.2026): the image carries /etc/modprobe.d/h713-arisc.conf with
firmware=vendor, its boot chain starts no crust (no scp in the U-Boot FIT
without the standby), and the device tree still names the board's crust
image, which stays in /lib/firmware as the experimental choice
(h713-arisc set crust).

Until now a missing h713-arisc.bin was harmless only because BL31 had
started crust: the driver kept the running crust (arisc_keep_without_file).
Without a crust in the boot chain there is nothing to keep, the probe
fails, and the HDMI front end (hot plug, EDID) stays without a firmware -
nothing hangs, since the driver maps none of the R_ block, but the input
is dead until a reinstall. h713-install refuses a dump without the file,
so this is the case of a file gone since, or of an image put together
by hand.

When the module option chose the vendor image and it cannot be loaded,
the driver now loads the device tree's image instead, with a warning,
and the state file says so (firmware_from=device tree, the vendor image
missing). A board whose tree names the vendor image itself is unchanged,
and so is a vendor image that loads but whose layout cannot be resolved
(the probe refuses it, as before).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
@@ -423,7 +423,7 @@
 
 	struct h713_arisc_offsets off;		/* resolved from the image */
 	const char		*fw_name;	/* the file it came from */
-	const char		*fw_from;	/* "device tree" or "module option" */
+	const char		*fw_from;	/* "device tree", "module option", or why not */
 	char			image_version[64];	/* its git describe */
 	u8			lane;		/* 0 or 3, see arisc_notify_version() */
 
@@ -3418,12 +3418,13 @@
 static int h713_arisc_start(struct h713_arisc *a)
 {
 	const struct firmware *fw;
-	const char *name = FW_NAME_ARISC;
+	const char *name = FW_NAME_ARISC, *dt_name;
 	bool kept;
 	int ret;
 
 	of_property_read_string_index(a->dev->of_node, "firmware-name", 0,
 				      &name);
+	dt_name = name;
 	a->fw_from = "device tree";
 	if (firmware && *firmware) {
 		if (!strcmp(firmware, "vendor") || !strcmp(firmware, FW_NAME_ARISC)) {
@@ -3456,6 +3457,20 @@
 
 	ret = request_firmware(&fw, name, a->dev);
 	/*
+	 * The option asked for the vendor image and it is not there - a dump
+	 * without it, or a file gone since: the device tree's crust image
+	 * instead, so that the HDMI front end keeps a firmware. An image whose
+	 * boot chain starts no crust (a release without the standby) has no
+	 * running crust that the code below could keep.
+	 */
+	if (ret && !strcmp(name, FW_NAME_ARISC) && strcmp(dt_name, FW_NAME_ARISC)) {
+		dev_warn(a->dev, "\"%s\" cannot be loaded (%d) -- loading the device tree's \"%s\" instead\n",
+			 name, ret, dt_name);
+		name = dt_name;
+		a->fw_from = "device tree, the vendor image missing";
+		ret = request_firmware(&fw, name, a->dev);
+	}
+	/*
 	 * A DT whose "sram" window stops short of crust's contract page is
 	 * older than this driver: through it crust can be neither loaded nor
 	 * kept, and nothing the DT does not describe is mapped. The vendor
